    <div class="jr-answer">
      <h2>The short answer</h2>
      <p>
        Yes — <strong>collagen for skin works, but only under specific conditions</strong>. Across
        19 controlled trials covering 1,125 people, hydrolysed collagen improved skin hydration,
        elasticity and wrinkle depth compared with placebo. What separates a supplement that works
        from one that does nothing comes down to four things:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Peptide size.</strong> Collagen has to be hydrolysed into fragments small enough to survive digestion and reach your bloodstream intact.</li>
        <li><strong>Dose.</strong> Most positive trials used 2.5g–10g of collagen peptides per day. Sachets delivering 500mg are below the threshold that has ever been studied.</li>
        <li><strong>Co-factors.</strong> Collagen synthesis is enzymatic. Without vitamin C and zinc, your body cannot assemble new collagen no matter how much you feed it.</li>
        <li><strong>Time.</strong> Skin turnover is roughly 28 days. Trials that measured elasticity found meaningful change at 8–12 weeks, not 8–12 days.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

