      <h2 id="why-skin-loses-collagen">Why your skin loses collagen</h2>

      <p>
        Collagen is the scaffolding of your dermis. Type I collagen alone accounts for roughly
        80–90% of the collagen in skin, arranged in dense fibre bundles that give skin its
        firmness and snap-back. When those bundles thin and fragment, the surface above them
        loses support — which is what you see as fine lines, crepiness and loss of definition
        along the jaw.
      </p>

      <p>
        The decline starts earlier than most people expect. Dermatological research going back to
        Shuster's foundational 1975 work has consistently found that skin collagen density falls
        by roughly <strong>1% per year from the mid-twenties onward</strong>. It is not a cliff at
        forty; it is a slow, compounding drawdown that has already been running for a decade before
        most people notice it.
      </p>

      <p>
        Two things accelerate it. The first is <strong>ultraviolet exposure</strong> — photoageing
        is estimated to account for up to 80% of visible facial ageing, because UV activates matrix
        metalloproteinases, the enzymes that cut collagen fibres apart. The second is
        <strong>oestrogen decline</strong>: skin collagen content drops sharply in the first five
        years after menopause, which is why so many women describe their skin changing character
        rather than simply ageing.
      </p>

