      <h2 id="does-it-survive-digestion">Does oral collagen survive digestion?</h2>

      <p>
        This is the objection worth taking seriously, and for a long time it was the correct one.
        Whole collagen is an enormous molecule — around 300,000 Daltons. Swallow it and your gut
        breaks it down into individual amino acids, which then go wherever your body needs amino
        acids. Nothing about that process is skin-specific.
      </p>

      <p>
        What changed the picture was the discovery that <strong>hydrolysed collagen behaves
        differently</strong>. When collagen is enzymatically cleaved into short peptide chains,
        a meaningful fraction survives digestion as intact di- and tripeptides. Iwai and colleagues
        demonstrated in 2005 that <strong>Pro-Hyp and Hyp-Gly appear in human blood</strong> after
        oral ingestion of collagen hydrolysate — measurable, circulating peptides rather than a
        generic amino acid pool.
      </p>

      <p>
        That distinction matters because those specific peptides appear to act as signalling
        molecules. Rather than being used as raw material, they are thought to reach the dermis and
        prompt fibroblasts — your collagen-producing cells — to increase output. The supplement is
        less a delivery of collagen than an instruction to make more of it.
      </p>

      <p>
        Which is why <strong>molecular weight is the single most useful number on a collagen
        label</strong>. Peptides below roughly 2,000 Daltons absorb efficiently. Products that
        list only "collagen" with no hydrolysation detail are typically sitting far above that
        threshold — and are, functionally, expensive protein powder.
      </p>

