      <h2 id="what-trials-found">What the clinical trials actually found</h2>

      <p>
        The most useful evidence is the 2021 systematic review and meta-analysis published in the
        <em>International Journal of Dermatology</em>, which pooled 19 randomised controlled trials
        covering 1,125 participants. Its conclusion: hydrolysed collagen supplementation produced
        statistically significant improvements in <strong>skin hydration, elasticity and wrinkle
        depth</strong> versus placebo.
      </p>

      <p>Read the underlying trials and a consistent shape emerges:</p>

      <ul>
        <li><strong>Hydration responds first</strong>, often measurable by week 4. It is also the effect with the largest effect size.</li>
        <li><strong>Elasticity follows at 8–12 weeks.</strong> This is the change people describe as skin feeling "springier" rather than looking different.</li>
        <li><strong>Wrinkle depth is the slowest and smallest effect.</strong> Real, statistically significant, but modest — collagen is not a substitute for a procedure.</li>
        <li><strong>Effective doses clustered between 2.5g and 10g daily.</strong> Below that range, the literature is essentially silent.</li>
      </ul>

      <p>
        It is worth being straightforward about the limitations. Many of these trials were
        industry-funded, sample sizes were often modest, and measurement methods varied between
        studies. The direction of effect is consistent and the mechanism is plausible — but anyone
        promising you a transformation in three weeks is selling something the evidence does not
        support.
      </p>

      <h2 id="type-i-vs-marine">Type I, marine vs bovine, and why source matters</h2>

      <p>
        There are 28 known collagen types; three matter for supplementation. <strong>Type I</strong>
        is the skin, bone and tendon collagen. <strong>Type II</strong> is cartilage-specific — the
        relevant type for <a href="/pages/collagen-for-joint-pain">joint support</a>.
        <strong>Type III</strong> sits alongside Type I in skin and blood vessels, more abundant in
        younger skin.
      </p>

      <p>
        For skin, you want Type I. Both marine and bovine collagen supply it, but they are not
        interchangeable in practice:
      </p>

