      </div>

      <p>
        This is why skin-focused formulas are usually marine-based. <a href="/products/collabloom">Collabloom</a>
        uses <strong>8,000mg of Japanese hydrolysed marine collagen</strong> per 30ml shot — 8g of
        protein, at the upper end of the doses used in published trials rather than the token
        amounts common in sachets.
      </p>

      <h2 id="cofactors">The co-factors that decide whether it works</h2>

      <p>
        Here is the part most collagen marketing skips. Building collagen is an enzymatic process,
        and the enzymes have hard requirements. Feeding your body peptides without those co-factors
        is like delivering bricks to a site with no mortar.
      </p>

      <h3>Vitamin C — non-negotiable</h3>
      <p>
        Prolyl and lysyl hydroxylase, the two enzymes that stabilise the collagen triple helix,
        require vitamin C as a cofactor. Without it, the helix does not cross-link properly and the
        collagen your body makes is structurally weak. This is not a marketing claim — it is the
        biochemistry behind scurvy. Any collagen supplement without vitamin C is working at a
        deficit by design.
      </p>

      <h3>Zinc — the enzyme switch</h3>
      <p>
        Zinc activates the enzymes that build collagen and helps regulate the matrix
        metalloproteinases that break it down. It works on both sides of the ledger.
      </p>

      <h3>Antioxidants — protecting what you just built</h3>
      <p>
        New collagen sitting in skin under daily UV and pollution exposure degrades on the same
        timeline it was built. Glutathione, vitamin E, lycopene and polyphenols such as pine bark
        extract reduce the oxidative load that drives that degradation. Glutathione in particular
        has a second role in skin: it inhibits tyrosinase, the enzyme that drives melanin
        production, which is why it appears in pigmentation protocols
        (<a href="/pages/glutathione-for-skin">more on that here</a>).
      </p>

      <h3>Hydration agents — the visible layer</h3>
      <p>
        Hyaluronic acid binds up to 1,000 times its weight in water, and oral ceramides support the
        skin barrier that stops that water escaping. These act faster than collagen itself and are
        usually responsible for the "my skin looks plumper" effect people report in the first month.
      </p>

