      <h2 id="how-long">How long before you see anything</h2>

      <p>
        Skin turnover takes roughly 28 days, and dermal remodelling is slower still. Anyone
        promising visible change in a fortnight is describing hydration, not collagen. A realistic
        timeline, based on how the trial data reads:
      </p>

      <ul>
        <li><strong>Weeks 1–4 — hydration.</strong> Skin feels less tight, makeup sits better. This is hyaluronic acid and ceramides doing their job.</li>
        <li><strong>Weeks 4–8 — texture and tone.</strong> Surface smoothness improves. Pigmentation begins to even out if your formula includes glutathione and vitamin C.</li>
        <li><strong>Weeks 8–12 — elasticity.</strong> The measurable dermal change. Skin recovers faster when pressed; fine lines soften at the edges.</li>
        <li><strong>Beyond 12 weeks — maintenance.</strong> Benefits plateau and hold while you continue. They fade over a few months if you stop, because the underlying ~1%-per-year decline resumes.</li>
      </ul>

      <p>
        The single biggest predictor of whether someone sees results is not the brand — it is
        whether they took it daily for twelve weeks. Consistency beats formulation, and formulation
        matters a great deal.
      </p>

      <h2 id="how-to-choose">How to read a collagen label</h2>

      <p>Five checks, in order of how much they matter:</p>

      <ol>
        <li><strong>Is a dose in milligrams stated?</strong> If the label says "collagen blend" without a number, assume the number is small. You want 2,500mg minimum; 8,000–10,000mg is the well-studied range.</li>
        <li><strong>Is it hydrolysed, and to what molecular weight?</strong> "Hydrolysed" should be explicit. Under 2,000 Daltons is the absorption benchmark.</li>
        <li><strong>Is vitamin C present?</strong> Without it, collagen synthesis is rate-limited regardless of dose.</li>
        <li><strong>What is the sugar content?</strong> Many collagen drinks carry 8–12g of sugar per serving. Dietary sugar drives glycation, which stiffens and damages existing collagen — actively working against the product.</li>
        <li><strong>Marine or bovine?</strong> Marine for skin. Bovine is fine, but it is a Type I/III blend optimised for something else.</li>
      </ol>

      <p>
        One honest caveat: collagen supplementation is a support, not a replacement for the basics.
        Daily broad-spectrum SPF prevents more collagen loss than any supplement can rebuild. If you
        are choosing between sunscreen and a collagen shot, choose the sunscreen. If you are already
        wearing sunscreen, a well-dosed collagen formula is the next meaningful step.
      </p>

