      <h2 id="faq">Frequently asked questions</h2>

      <div class="jr-faq">
        <details>
          <summary>How much collagen should I take per day for skin?</summary>
          <p>
            Published trials showing skin benefits used between 2.5g and 10g of hydrolysed collagen
            peptides daily. Doses below 2.5g have little supporting evidence. Collabloom delivers
            8,000mg (8g) per 30ml shot, which sits in the well-studied upper range.
          </p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Is marine collagen better than bovine collagen for skin?</summary>
          <p>
            For skin specifically, yes. Marine collagen is almost entirely Type I — the dominant
            collagen in the dermis — and its naturally smaller peptide structure hydrolyses to a
            lower molecular weight, which is generally reported to absorb faster and more completely
            than bovine collagen.
          </p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>When is the best time to take a collagen supplement?</summary>
          <p>
            Consistency matters far more than timing. There is no strong evidence favouring morning
            over evening. Many people take it on an empty stomach in the morning simply because it
            is easier to remember, and daily adherence is the strongest predictor of results.
          </p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Can collagen supplements cause weight gain or breakouts?</summary>
          <p>
            Collagen itself is a protein with negligible calories at supplement doses — Collabloom
            is 35 kcal per shot with zero sugar. Breakouts are more commonly linked to the sugar,
            dairy or flavouring in poorly formulated collagen drinks than to collagen peptides.
          </p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Do I need to keep taking collagen forever?</summary>
          <p>
            Benefits are maintained while you supplement and gradually fade over several months
            after stopping, because the underlying age-related decline of roughly 1% per year
            resumes. Think of it as ongoing support rather than a fixed course.
          </p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Can I take collagen with vitamin C tablets separately?</summary>
          <p>
            You can, and it is better than no vitamin C at all. A formula with both included is
            simpler and ensures they are present together at the point of absorption. Collabloom
            includes 250mg of vitamin C from amla extract alongside its collagen.
          </p>
        </details>
      </div>

